---
/* GIARRE — the town a document names and the registers do not confirm.
 *
 * This page exists to hold a NEGATIVE, which is the harder kind of page to
 * write and the more useful kind to have. Giovanni Nicotra's own marriage
 * certificate says he was born here. Eleven years of Giarre births say he was
 * not. Both statements are read, and the page's job is to leave them standing
 * beside each other rather than quietly preferring one.
 */
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import P from "../components/P.astro";
import { u } from "../lib/url.js";
import holdings from "../data/holdings.json";
import coverage from "../data/coverage.json";
import nic from "../data/nicotra.json";

const HERE = "Giarre";
const rows = holdings.rows.filter((r) => r.comune.startsWith(HERE));
const cov = coverage.rows.find((r) => r.comune === HERE) || {};
const vols = rows.reduce((n, r) => n + r.volumes, 0);
const nati = rows.find((r) => r.type === "Nati") || {};
const morti = rows.find((r) => r.type === "Morti") || {};
const matr = rows.find((r) => r.type === "Matrimoni") || {};
const proc = rows.find((r) => /processetti/.test(r.type)) || {};
const YEARS = nic.byComune["Giarre"] || [];
const HOUSE = (nic.housesByComune["Giarre"] || [])[0] || {};

---
<Base title="Giarre" eyebrow="Catania, Sicily"
      dek="A town named by a document, contradicted by eleven of its own years, and then answered by its first. The largest comune in this archive.">

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">21 September 2026 · «nato a Giarre»</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">Six words on a printed form</h2>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <p>Piedimonte's <em>Matrimoni allegati</em> of 1872, fascicolo 20, holds the publication
      certificate for <strong>Giovanni Nicotra</strong> and <strong>Venera Cassaniti</strong>. The
      1872 form prints <em>«nato a ____»</em> separately from <em>«residente a ____»</em>, and the
      clerk filled both:</p>
      <blockquote style="border-left:3px solid var(--rule);padding-left:1rem;margin:1rem 0;font-family:var(--serif);color:var(--ink-2)">
        TRA <strong>GIOVANNI NICOTRA</strong> — nato a <strong>GIARRE</strong> — residente a
        <strong>MASCALI</strong> — di professione bracciante — di età anni trentuno
      </blockquote>
      <p>That sentence is why this comune is in the archive at all. It is also, so far, the only
      evidence that anybody in this family was ever here.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>Eleven years read end to end, and he is in none of them</h2>
    <p class="dek" style="margin:.7rem 0 1.2rem">Giarre's annual <em>Tavola</em> is alphabetical and
    names both parents, so a Nicotra of a Rosario would identify itself without an act being opened.</p>
    <div class="scroll">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th class="m">Year</th><th>The N section</th><th>What is around it</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {YEARS.map((r) => (
            <tr>
              <td class="m"><strong>{r.year}</strong></td>
              <td class={["none","empty","\u2014"].includes(r.entries) ? "small" : ""}>
                {["none","empty","\u2014"].includes(r.entries) ? r.entries : <strong>{r.entries}</strong>}</td>
              <td class="small">{r.note}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note" style="margin-top:16px"><strong>The only Nicotra household at Giarre in eleven
    years is a titled one.</strong> <em>Dottor Don</em> Alfio Nicotra and <em>Donna</em> Antonina
    Consoli name a Salvatore in November 1846 and a Salvatore again in January 1848 — which is what a
    family does when the first child dies. <strong>Giovanni's father was a bracciante.</strong>
    Different household, different condition.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">22 September 2026 · found in another town's register</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">There were untitled Nicotra at Giarre after all</h2>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <p>The eleven years above are still eleven years, and they still do not hold Giovanni. But the
      sentence they seemed to support — that Giarre's Nicotra were <em>all</em> gentry — was never
      what they said. It was what eleven years of one register could not see.</p>
      <p><a href={u("/riposto/")}>Riposto</a>'s marriage <em>Tavola</em> for <strong>1844</strong>,
      atto 39, marries <strong>Nicotra Salvadore</strong> to Maria Barbagallo. The <em>patria</em>
      column gives his as <strong>GIARRE</strong>. His parents are written plainly, with no
      <em>Don</em> and no <em>Donna</em>: <strong>Alfio Nicotra and Rosa Pappalardo</strong>. He then
      registers two children at Riposto, in 1845 and 1847.</p>
      <p><strong>That is an ordinary Giarre Nicotra family, documented — and invisible in Giarre's own
      birth indexes.</strong> Salvadore married in 1844, so he was born about 1815 to 1824, which is
      before the earliest year this archive had read here. It does not find Giovanni. It does
      something better for the argument: it shows that a Nicotra can be <em>nato a Giarre</em>, be
      entirely absent from the Giarre years that have been read, and turn up in Riposto's registers
      instead — <strong>which is exactly what Giovanni's certificate and Giarre's silence together
      require of him</strong>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">22 September 2026 · Giarre, Nati 1820, the first year of all</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">The decade was wrong, not the town</h2>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <p>Salvadore Nicotra's marriage pointed at a birth around 1815–1824, so the first Giarre volume
      was opened: <strong>1820</strong>, the year civil registration began in Sicily at all — there is
      nothing before it anywhere south of the strait. Its N section was read whole.</p>
      <p><strong>It holds two Nicotra households, and neither carries a title.</strong></p>
      <div class="rec" style="margin-top:12px">
        <div class="k">atto 198</div><div class="v"><strong>Nicotra Rosaria</strong> — of <strong>Nicotra Simone</strong> and Marianna Dadone</div><div class="w">13 April 1820</div>
        <div class="k">atto 228</div><div class="v"><strong>Nicotra Carmela</strong> — of <strong>Nicotra Rosario</strong> and Rosa Providenza</div><div class="w">1 May 1820</div>
      </div>
        <figure style="margin-top:1.4rem">
        <img src={u("/plates/giarre1820-nati-nicotra.jpg")} alt="Giarre, Nati 1820 — the N section, with two Nicotra households"
             style="width:100%;height:auto;border:1px solid var(--rule)" loading="lazy" />
        <figcaption class="dek" style="margin-top:.6rem">Giarre, Stato civile della restaurazione, <strong>Nati 1820</strong>, the annual <em>Tavola</em> at the N section — Archivio di Stato di Catania (Antenati <em>an_ua68978</em>, image 553). <strong>198 · Nicotra Rosaria</strong> — Nicotra Simone / Dadone Marianna, 13 April. <strong>228 · Nicotra Carmela</strong> — <strong>Nicotra Rosario</strong> / Providenza Rosa, 1 May. Below them Nucifora, Nicolosi and Nota, and then O. No <em>Don</em> and no <em>Donna</em> on the page.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p style="margin-top:16px">So the sentence this page carried for a day — that Giarre's Nicotra
      are gentry — was never a fact about Giarre. <strong>It was a fact about eleven years of
      it.</strong> Eighteen years earlier the surname is here twice over in one volume, written plainly,
      the way a labourer's name is written.</p>
      <p><strong>And the second father is called Rosario</strong>, which is the name this archive has
      been hunting for a week. <strong>It is not him.</strong> Giovanni's father was a working
      bracciante in October 1872; a man fathering a child in May 1820 would be well past seventy-five
      by then. What a Rosario Nicotra of Giarre in 1820 <em>is</em> is the generation a grandson would
      be named after — and <a href={u("/method/")}>naming custom is evidence, but weak evidence</a>,
      and this archive has been burned by treating it as more. The name is recorded, the join is not
      made, and the way to test it is <strong>Salvadore Nicotra's own birth act</strong>, which would
      say whether Alfio, Simone and Rosario are brothers.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <div class="lab">a caution this comune taught</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">The alphabet starts again after Z</h2>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <p>The 1848 index says so itself, in the clerk's own hand, in the <em>Osservazioni</em> column
      beside the Nicotra entry: <em>«in fine dell'alfabeto riportata altra volta la lettera M»</em> —
      at the end of the alphabet the letter M is entered a second time.</p>
      <p><strong>Giarre's Tavole append extra letter-sections after Z</strong> for acts registered
      late. Nine of the eleven years do it; 1846 and 1847 do not, and end at Z with a blank leaf. So a
      volume with no appendix <strong>looks exactly like a volume whose appendix you have not
      reached</strong>, and «the alphabet finished» is not the same as «the index finished». Every one
      of the eleven years above has had its tail read as well as its main run.
      <a href={u("/method/")}>The instruments →</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk">
    <h2>What Giarre holds</h2>
    <p class="dek" style="margin:.7rem 0 1.2rem">{vols} volumes — the largest holding of any comune in
    this archive, and the listing that proved <code>s_size=500</code> is a page and not a ceiling.</p>
    <div class="scroll">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Fondo</th><th>Record type</th><th class="m">Years</th><th class="m">Volumes</th><th class="m">Openable</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {rows.map((r) => (
            <tr>
              <td class="small">{r.fondo}</td>
              <td><strong>{r.type}</strong></td>
              <td class="m small">{r.from ? `${r.from}–${r.to}` : "—"}</td>
              <td class="m">{r.volumes}</td>
              <td class="m small">{r.bought || "—"}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p class="note" style="margin-top:16px"><strong>Giarre holds two copies of every year.</strong>
    Each register appears twice under different <em>segnature</em> — {nati.volumes} Nati volumes for
    roughly half that many years, and the same doubling in {morti.volumes} Morti and {matr.volumes}
    Matrimoni. <strong>A leaf lost in one copy may survive in the other</strong>, which is a resource
    this archive has nowhere else, and has not yet needed to use. Unlike
    <a href={u("/mascali/")}>Mascali</a> it does have 1844.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="blk" style="border-bottom:0">
    <h2>Three readings, and none of them proved</h2>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <p>Giovanni Nicotra's certificate says Giarre. Giarre's registers, read for eleven consecutive
      years in both the main run and the appendix, do not hold him. <strong>Something has to give, and
      this archive does not yet know what.</strong></p>
      <ul style="font-family:var(--sans);font-size:14px;line-height:1.75;color:var(--ink-2);padding-left:20px">
        <li><strong>His ages are wrong again.</strong> He is <em>trentuno</em> in October 1872 and
        <em>ventotto</em> in December 1875 — already six years apart in three. The windows either side
        of the read decade are 1833–37 and 1849–53, and neither has been touched.</li>
        <li><strong>He was registered at <a href={u("/riposto/")}>Riposto</a></strong>, the lower town,
        which has registered separately since 1821. <strong>This reading now has a case of its own
        behind it</strong>: Nicotra Salvadore, <em>patria Giarre</em>, married at Riposto in 1844 and
        registered his children there. It is still not Giovanni, and one man's habit is not a rule —
        but it is no longer an inference from general history alone.</li>
        <li><strong>«Giarre» is loose for a frazione</strong> — Macchia, Trepunti, Altarello, San
        Giovanni Montebello. <strong>This one now has a documented precedent</strong>, below.</li>
      </ul>
      <p style="margin-top:14px">Nothing above is a finding. It is what is left when a document and a
      register disagree and both have been read. <a href={u("/nicotra/")}>The Nicotra line →</a> · <a href={u("/open-questions/")}>The open questions →</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="blk" style="border-bottom:0">
    <div class="lab">22 September 2026 · two registers, one man, two birthplaces</div>
    <h2 style="margin-top:.4rem">The same man is «of S. Giovanni» here and «of Giarre» there</h2>
    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:1rem">
      <p>Giarre's marriage <em>Tavola</em> for <strong>1844</strong> holds the wedding of the
      <em>Dottor Don</em> himself — <strong>atto 54, 19 August 1844: Nicotra D.<sup>r</sup> D. Alfio
      of Giarre, son of Nicotra D. Giuseppe and Caltabiano D. Gioachina, marrying Consoli D.
      Antonina.</strong> They are the couple whose Salvatore appears in the birth index in 1846 and
      again in 1848.</p>
      <p>Six years earlier, <strong>Riposto</strong>'s marriage Tavola records <strong>atto 15, 23
      April 1838: Nicotra D. Alfio, son of <em>fu</em> D. Giuseppe and D. Gioachina Cattabiano,
      marrying Ligresti D. Angela.</strong> <strong>The same two parents.</strong> It is one man,
      widowed and remarried — not two namesakes, which is what this archive had written down while it
      had only one of the acts.</p>
        <figure style="margin-top:1.4rem">
        <img src={u("/plates/giarre1844-matr54-don-alfio.jpg")} alt="Giarre, Matrimoni 1844, atto 54 — Nicotra D.r D. Alfio"
             style="width:100%;height:auto;border:1px solid var(--rule)" loading="lazy" />
        <figcaption class="dek" style="margin-top:.6rem">Giarre, <strong>Matrimoni 1844</strong>, the annual <em>Tavola</em>, <strong>atto 54</strong> — Archivio di Stato di Catania (Antenati <em>an_ua68872</em>, image 162). «<strong>Nicotra D.<sup>r</sup> D. Alfio</strong> — <em>Giarre</em> — <strong>Nicotra D. Giuseppe / Caltabiano D. Gioachina</strong>» marrying «Consoli D. Antonina — Consoli D. Biaggio…», 19 August. Those two parents are the same pair Riposto wrote for its Don Alfio six years earlier, which is what joins the two acts into one man.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <p>And the two registers give him <strong>two different birthplaces</strong>. Riposto's clerk
      wrote his <em>patria</em> as <strong>«S. Giovanni»</strong>. Giarre's wrote
      <strong>«Giarre»</strong>. <strong>San Giovanni Montebello is a frazione of Giarre</strong> —
      so both are true, and the difference is only which name the clerk reached for.</p>
      <p><strong>That is the third reading, documented, in this family's own surname.</strong> It does
      not prove anything about Giovanni Nicotra. It proves the thing his case needs to be possible:
      that a man of this district can be <em>«nato a Giarre»</em> in one register and entered under a
      frazione's name in another, and that a reader who trusts one word will look in the wrong book.
      <a href={u("/riposto/")}>Riposto →</a> · <a href={u("/nicotra/")}>The Nicotra line →</a></p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
